---
scope: src/startServer
alignment: unreviewed
description: Runs the AIDE MCP server for one project, advertising every aide tool with what agents may pass and handing each call to the tool that does the work.
intent: >
  This is the doorway agents walk through to reach AIDE. It starts the server
  for a project, tells the connecting agent which tools exist, when and how to
  call each one and which choices each accepts, then hands every call to the
  right tool and returns that tool's answer as the reply. Success means an agent
  can use every capability the package offers from the advertisement alone, and
  every call lands where it was meant with what the agent sent. It loads only
  when the server runs, so the package's terminal commands stay fast.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Every tool the package offers is advertised, each with a description that teaches an agent when to call it and what it gets back.
    - The choices each tool advertises, such as its category or type lists, match exactly what the tool accepts, with none missing and none extra.
    - Each call reaches the tool it names with the agent's arguments unchanged, and that tool's answer, refusals included, comes back as the reply.
    - The server identifies itself to the connecting agent with the package's own name and version.
    - The package's terminal commands run without loading the server or any tool.
  undesired:
    - A tool or a choice the package accepts is missing from the advertisement, so agents never learn they can use it.
    - The advertisement offers a tool or a choice that the tool then refuses.
    - A call is routed to the wrong tool, or its arguments are dropped, renamed or altered on the way.
    - A tool's refusal reaches the agent looking like a success, so the agent carries on as if the work was done.
    - A terminal command is slowed because it pulls in the server and every tool it advertises.
---
